{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,240p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed1594776539/15/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"db118e2a\" lines=\"7-23\">\n<description>\nNormalize the framed input motif into a 5x5 icon and stamp two copies on a 15x15 canvas.\n\nInput structure\nThe input is a rounded rectangular frame: the four corners are the background\ncolor, and every other outer-border pixel has one frame color. Non-background\npixels inside that frame are the motif to preserve.\n\nRule\nMake a 5x5 version of the rounded frame with a 3x3 interior. Crop the input\ninterior motif to its bounding box, center that crop in the 3x3 interior, and\npaste the resulting 5x5 icon at upper-left positions (2, 2) and (8, 8) in a\n15x15 background canvas.\n\nKey insight\nThe variable-size frame is only a carrier; the meaningful object is the\ncompact interior motif, which is normalized to the fixed 3x3 icon interior.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"332202d5\" lines=\"49-64\">\n<description>\nFill every row from the nearest marked horizontal bar, turning bar rows and color-boundary ties into separator rows.\n\nInput structure\n- A dominant background fills the grid except for full-width horizontal bars and one vertical marker column.\n- Outside the marker column, every row is monochrome: either background or a source bar color.\n- Background rows share one marker color in the marker column; source bar rows share the other.\n\nRule\n- Each non-source row takes the color of the nearest source bar, with that row's marker-column pixel set to the source-marker color.\n- If a row is exactly midway between nearest bars of different colors, make the whole row the source-marker color.\n- Each original source bar row becomes a source-marker row with the marker-column pixel changed to the background-row marker color.\n\nKey insight\n- The marked bars are horizontal seeds for a one-dimensional nearest-seed partition over rows; equal-distance rows are separators only when the competing seed colors differ.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"e3fe1151\" lines=\"36-55\">\n<description>\nFill the one blank in each corner region so all four regions have the same color multiset.\n\nInput structure:\n- The dominant background color forms one solid separator row and one solid\n  separator column.\n- The separator cross splits the grid into four equal rectangular corner regions.\n- Each corner region contains exactly one background-colored blank pixel.\n\nRule:\n- Count the non-background colors in each corner region.\n- The completed region multiset is defined by the maximum observed count of\n  each color across the four regions.\n- Replace each region's blank pixel with the single color needed to match that\n  completed multiset.\n\nKey insight:\n- The separator color inside a corner region is a missing value marker; comparing\n  the four regions reveals the shared multiset without using numeric color order.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"e45ef808\" lines=\"31-46\">\n<description>\nMark the shallowest and deepest columns of a solid terrain boundary with vertical colored guide lines.\n\nInput structure:\n- The first row is a solid empty separator.\n- Below it, each column consists of upper fill cells followed by a contiguous suffix of terrain cells.\n- The bottom row is all terrain, so every column has a well-defined first terrain row.\n\nRule:\n- Find the column whose first terrain row is latest; it is unique and its upper fill run becomes maroon.\n- Find the rightmost column whose first terrain row is earliest; its upper fill run becomes yellow.\n- Preserve the empty separator, terrain cells, and all other upper fill cells.\n\nKey insight:\n- The task is a column-height summary of the terrain profile: color the deepest upper run and the highest terrain peak.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"ba1aa698\" lines=\"41-55\">\n<description>\nExtrapolate the moving sprite from a horizontal sequence of shared-border frames into the next standalone frame.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid is a row of equal-width framed panels sharing their vertical border columns.\n- Each panel has the same fill color and one identical interior sprite at the same local columns.\n- The sprite's top row changes by a constant amount from panel to panel.\n\nRule\n- Build one output panel with the same height, frame color, fill color, and sprite.\n- Continue the sprite's diagonal motion one more panel to the right.\n- Because the output is a standalone panel, advance by the full panel width, not the shared-border spacing.\n\nKey insight\n- The panels are consecutive frames of motion; the output is the next frame after the sequence.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"c803e39c\" lines=\"31-40\">\n<description>\nExpand the second mask into a block grid by stamping the first mask with the two payload colors.\n\nInput structure\n- A background grid has four panels separated by three full-height separator columns.\n- The first two panels contain same-size binary masks; the last two contain solid same-size color squares.\nRule\n- Use the second mask as an outer layout. For each foreground cell in that outer mask, place a tile equal to the first mask, coloring its foreground with the first solid-square color and its background with the second solid-square color; outer background cells become all second solid-square color.\nKey insight\n- The output is the Kronecker product of the two binary masks, with the two right-hand panel colors providing the palette.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"9b5080bb\" lines=\"161-172\">\n<description>\nRecolor the small defect bars on each shared-color object with the opposite panel color.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid contains two large panel colors and one shared object color.\n- Each shared-color object is a mostly solid rectangle inside exactly one panel.\nRule\n- Find the near-solid rectangular core of each object.\n- Missing core pixels and one-cell protrusions define two-pixel bars perpendicular to the nearest core edge.\n- Recolor those bars with the other panel color; leave all other pixels unchanged.\nKey insight\n- The colors to add are not tied to numeric ids: each object's surrounding panel identifies the opposite panel color.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"5dbc8537\" lines=\"10-23\">\n<description>\nFill rectangular cavities in a template shape by placing source sprites into matching holes.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. A dominant \"base\" color forms a large frame/template with rectangular gaps.\n- Small colored sprites sit outside the template region.\n\nRule:\n- The base color (largest connected component excluding bg) defines the template bbox.\n- Each rectangular hole inside the template matches exactly one outside sprite by size.\n- Place each sprite into its matching cavity; output is the filled template bbox.\n\nKey insight: Sprite bounding boxes match cavity dimensions exactly — it's a jigsaw-fit puzzle where each piece has a unique slot.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"54dc2872\" lines=\"52-69\">\n<description>\nMove each marked L-shaped sprite onto the separate pixel matching its marker color.\n\nInput structure\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- Foreground has 4-connected multicolor sprites and isolated one-pixel controls.\n- Each sprite has one repeated body color in an L shape plus one embedded marker\n  pixel of a different color.\n\nRule\n- If the embedded marker color also appears as a separate isolated control pixel,\n  translate the whole sprite so the body L-bend lands on that control pixel.\n- Clear each moved sprite from its old position, clear its consumed control pixel,\n  then paste all translated sprites; sprites without a matching control stay put.\n\nKey insight\n- The marker color pairs a sprite with its destination control, while the L-bend\n  gives the orientation-independent anchor cell to place on that destination.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"1a244afd\" lines=\"49-68\">\n<description>\nRotate each magenta source pixel 90 degrees counterclockwise around its matched blue pivot, recoloring the new pixel orange.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- The only foreground colors are blue pivot pixels and magenta source pixels.\n- Each magenta source can be paired one-to-one with a blue pivot in the same\n  row or column.\n\nRule:\n- Remove all magenta source pixels.\n- For each matched source/pivot pair, preserve the source distance from the\n  pivot but rotate its offset 90 degrees counterclockwise.\n- Paint the rotated in-bounds target orange; if the target is outside the\n  grid, the source simply disappears.\n\nKey insight:\nThe blue pixels are rotation centers, and row/column alignment gives a\nunique global matching from each magenta source to its pivot.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"fe45cba4\" lines=\"72-89\">\n<description>\nMove the left-edge fragment into its same-colored anchor fragment to complete a solid rectangle.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid has a dominant background color.\n- Exactly one foreground color is split into two 4-connected objects: one\n  touches the left edge, and the other is an anchor away from the left edge.\n- The translated left-edge object and the anchor can form one filled rectangle.\nRule\n- Find the unique split color whose left-edge object can be translated onto\n  background cells beside the other same-colored object so their union is a\n  solid axis-aligned rectangle.\n- Clear the left-edge object's original cells to the background.\n- Paint that object at the unique completing offset, preserving all other cells.\nKey insight\n- The active color is identified by object structure and rectangle completion,\n  not by a fixed color id; the same-colored anchor object stays in place.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"12\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"22425bda\" lines=\"58-76\">\n<description>\nList the colors of overlaid straight lines in their only possible bottom-to-top crossing order.\n\nInput structure\nThe background is the dominant color. Every foreground color is the visible part\nof one full one-cell-thick horizontal, vertical, or 45-degree diagonal line.\nHigher lines may hide pixels of lower lines at crossings.\n\nRule\nRecover each full line from its visible collinear pixels. At any cell where\nmultiple recovered lines cross, the visible color is above every other line\nthrough that cell. Return the unique ordering of all line colors that respects\nthose above/below constraints and whose consecutive colors are lines that\ngeometrically cross.\n\nKey insight\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
